            <ArticleTitle>Anxiety states: relationship to atmospheric cations and serotonin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>262-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Five cases are described that offer evidence for the existence of the &quot;serotonin irritation syndrome,&quot; an anxiety state associated with high cation environments, elevated serum serotonin, and decreased urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid. The therapeutic response and reduction in serum serotonin after treatment with methysergide and/or removal from the high-cation environment support a serotonergic basis of the anxiety.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
